Simon Richard Clarkstone wrote:
> azgor wrote:
> > [5]: Red dancing shoes. Applying them three times teleports you to
> > Home1. --shel-
>
> IIRC, this originated from one of my suggestions on-channel.  The
> teleportation effect was not what I meant; I was not referring to _The
> Wizard of Oz_, but to the disturbing fairy tale:
>
> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Red_Shoes_%28fairy_tale%29
>
> They would auto-curse, and you would be unable to stop dancing in them.
>   In the fairy tale, they have to be removed by the girl's feet being
> chopped off, but I think the Dungeons will have a better way (i.e. the
> standard cursed-boots-removal mechanisms).
>
> Possibly they could try to stop you going to altars/temples or even try
> to stop you praying for their removal, to fit with the source material,
> but this may be too complex or unbalancing to be implemented.

Very interesting. I have to say I like the idea of adding references to
folk tales and fables into nethack. While many of hethacks's sources
(tolkien, D&D etc) draw on these sources, nh doesn't seem to draw on
them directly very often. Many of the folk tales (particularly the
Germanic ones) in their original, undisneyfied forms, seem to be
suitably eccentric, magical, recognisable and gory for consideration as
nethack patches



